#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054514 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054716 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "70b2f3c7-1600-51c6-91ad-855bcee7d18a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Coordinates of a Point on the Unit Circle Given Its Central Angle"}] :question/id #uuid "c4d9963b-1fed-523f-89c7-961ffb12feee" :question/math-academy-id "e-3343" :question/problem "![](images/3f/3f162b6b0c51a94c3c752f8a6ee59d4232c5bbb2fe619c7c6273f37805c281b0.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. What are the coordinates of the point $P?$" :question/worked-solution "Any point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta$ as follows:\n\n$$\nx = \\cos\\theta, \\qquad y = \\sin\\theta.\n$$\n\nWe're given that $\\theta=45^\\circ$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡{45}^{∘}=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}, \\\\ y & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡{45}^{∘}=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the coordinates of $P$ are $\\left(\\dfrac{\\sqrt 2}{2},\\dfrac{\\sqrt 2}{2}\\right).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054515 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054717 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "568011de-56c0-55f4-95ec-0a426cbf6b67" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Central Angle Given the Coordinates of a Point on the Unit Circle"}] :question/id #uuid "59696ddf-7aa4-561a-ac77-2216038294b2" :question/math-academy-id "e-3346" :question/problem "![](images/56/56adc0846dcc0a0f6352eb4ce411f4fffdcd6f91e20524c83bd70af1bd4d948c.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $y$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\dfrac{\\sqrt 3}{2},$ what is the measure of the angle $\\theta?$" :question/worked-solution "We're given that $y=\\dfrac{\\sqrt 3}{2}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\sin\\theta = \\dfrac{\\sqrt 3}{2}.\n$$\n\nWe compute the angle $\\theta$ using the inverse sine:\n\n$$\n\\theta = \\arcsin\\left(\\dfrac{\\sqrt 3}{2}\\right) = 60^\\circ.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054516 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054719 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "83a4297b-016b-5182-bf4f-cd2b64a23c41" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Tangent or Cotangent of a Central Angle Given a Point on the Unit Circle"}] :question/id #uuid "ee188df6-0ef5-5819-8fd9-77d48e8a5dce" :question/math-academy-id "e-3347" :question/problem "![](images/1b/1b084e0ad229e935d70d751f0e0ddaacf5832017951a7ef8e334f27834662d5c.png)\n\nThe point $P\\left( \\dfrac{\\sqrt 7}{7},\\dfrac{\\sqrt{42}}{7}\\right)$ lies on the unit circle, as shown. What is $\\tan\\theta?$" :question/worked-solution "We're given that $x=\\dfrac{\\sqrt 7}{7}$ and $y=\\dfrac{\\sqrt{42}}{7}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\cos\\theta = \\dfrac{\\sqrt 7}{7}, \\qquad \\sin\\theta =\\dfrac{\\sqrt{42}}{7}.\n$$\n\nKnowing that $\\tan\\theta = \\dfrac{\\sin \\theta}{\\cos\\theta},$ we can calculate $\\tan\\theta$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\tan\\theta = \\dfrac{\\left(\\dfrac{\\sqrt{42}}{7}\\right)}{\\left(\\dfrac{\\sqrt 7}{7}\\right)} = \\dfrac{\\sqrt{42} }{\\sqrt{7}} = \\sqrt 6.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054517 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054718 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8487f45b-a734-5375-abc2-5a347d49587c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Reciprocal Trigonometric Ratio Given the Coordinates of a Point on the Unit Circle"}] :question/id #uuid "acb093c8-daa1-5eee-8678-e985fb9dfd71" :question/math-academy-id "e-3348" :question/problem "![](images/16/1635b5c2b1c515601e5339a0814132fca63164e7ab2b45c93ea5c2b3f5e0358b.png)\n\nThe point $P\\left( \\dfrac{\\sqrt 8}{3},\\dfrac{1}{3}\\right)$ lies on the unit circle, as shown. What is $\\sec\\theta?$" :question/worked-solution "We're given that $x=\\dfrac{\\sqrt 8}{3}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\cos\\theta = \\dfrac{\\sqrt 8}{3}.\n$$\n\nKnowing that $\\sec\\theta = \\dfrac{1}{\\cos\\theta},$ we can calculate $\\sec\\theta$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\sec\\theta = \\dfrac{1}{\\left(\\dfrac{\\sqrt 8}{3}\\right)} =\\dfrac{3}{\\sqrt 8} = \\dfrac{3\\sqrt 8}{8}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535064 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054719 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "83a4297b-016b-5182-bf4f-cd2b64a23c41" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Tangent or Cotangent of a Central Angle Given a Point on the Unit Circle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3f8a243e-b4f0-5412-8987-90fb7ae767aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534385} {:answer/id #uuid "43c32df9-34ff-54be-aa25-f9bd5b25645e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534392} {:answer/id #uuid "53aa5f4f-ff96-5d4f-b815-838e91c9c3a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534431} {:answer/id #uuid "7e14b85b-2e42-5d8b-88e9-156a24dbe1a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534537} {:answer/id #uuid "a31109d2-e5c2-5154-823a-edd90f36b36c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534634}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "53aa5f4f-ff96-5d4f-b815-838e91c9c3a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534431} :answer-field/id #uuid "c7654d5f-ce50-5f5a-bb2d-57f8b6d0f4f3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534941}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "be261196-18fe-5211-8ddf-c96cd6bddf72" :question/math-academy-id "q-124387" :question/problem "![](images/c5/c5b92e2126df555db5f639bc9063586b6afc3bb2896b14edadedf183ec152ac5.png)\n\nThe point $P(\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3},\\frac{1}{3})$ lies on the unit circle, as shown. What is $\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta ?$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/df/df5d54940a136a53c1e1c00f8cc9d71d7b064f6e0895a029da2738cf21998a56.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $x=\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3}$ and $y=\\frac{1}{3}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nKnowing that $\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta },$ we can calculate $\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta =\\frac{(\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3})}{(\\frac{1}{3})}=2\\sqrt{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535067 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054716 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "70b2f3c7-1600-51c6-91ad-855bcee7d18a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Coordinates of a Point on the Unit Circle Given Its Central Angle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f654ae8d-650e-57f6-9611-fb6896357adc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.94" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534814}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f654ae8d-650e-57f6-9611-fb6896357adc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.94" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534814} :answer-field/id #uuid "cf419f8a-b3c5-543e-8006-abfeca431d5d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534946}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c979c575-4198-50eb-b74a-9d92d0b93552" :question/math-academy-id "q-246761" :question/problem "![](images/1d/1d1cf52a210aba670c697863822f0534669127d178e8df13d4aecfe8adf01df8.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows a unit circle. Rounding to two significant figures, the $x$-coordinate of the point $P$ is{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "![](images/eb/eba7dd6d571c989a14900a91eb432a216b74717046bfaa19e5fbe4de7abe6503.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $\\theta ={20}^{∘}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡{20}^{∘}=0.939\\,69…≈0.94.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534982 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054718 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8487f45b-a734-5375-abc2-5a347d49587c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Reciprocal Trigonometric Ratio Given the Coordinates of a Point on the Unit Circle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "667b63c5-fe72-5337-ad38-8eb1ffe5a17b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\sqrt{6}}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534473}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "667b63c5-fe72-5337-ad38-8eb1ffe5a17b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\sqrt{6}}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534473} :answer-field/id #uuid "98724bad-e3c1-526b-a661-1f3e637be2df" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534916}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1ba23b8d-4b13-599f-bc74-567006cafd11" :question/math-academy-id "q-267390" :question/problem "![](images/8f/8feb78ada73af4c97e7ce60555cf22acf5e24da38e30424c4c5c430e6456c06d.png)\n\nThe point $P(\\frac{1}{5},\\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{5})$ lies on the unit circle, as shown. What is $\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta ?$ Do not round your answer.\n\n$\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta =$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "![](images/41/41c6badfbcafe7bafdddca30606511d2d6c3c8bdbbcd5ec2e29ff9c4c2dd310f.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $y=\\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{5}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{5}.\n$$\n\nKnowing that $\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta },$ we can calculate $\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{5})}=\\frac{5}{2\\sqrt{6}}=\\frac{5\\sqrt{6}}{12}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535005 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054716 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "70b2f3c7-1600-51c6-91ad-855bcee7d18a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Coordinates of a Point on the Unit Circle Given Its Central Angle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ec93734-0bdb-5eb4-9807-7caf5ce894d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =-\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534295} {:answer/id #uuid "27ee5dd9-b7eb-5a9e-b074-10bf5bd72fa8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534347} {:answer/id #uuid "56f29b1c-7e49-54b7-80d6-51a39d5b565e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{5}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534440} {:answer/id #uuid "9093f9a2-7f99-5977-8d45-aec38c8f9e44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534585} {:answer/id #uuid "d2fcd0af-cffa-5e74-ae4d-8d682e1820e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =-\\frac{5}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534739}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "56f29b1c-7e49-54b7-80d6-51a39d5b565e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{5}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534440} :answer-field/id #uuid "1cab1b94-4dfd-5dc6-8b3a-13e1a63ed95c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534848}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "47def0cf-3ef3-5e5d-b5dc-5aca43c3b9ae" :question/math-academy-id "q-37891" :question/problem "![](images/5f/5f64637e42c8117ac66501a06470aa805ae3cf15fddf7840317394db9acb470f.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows a unit circle. Given that the $y$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\frac{5}{6},$ which of the following is correct?" :question/worked-solution "![](images/a9/a935d38f0306fe713c997a2904267356359fdcd8f14ca5b655e0c420a5756708.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $y=\\frac{5}{6}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{5}{6}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055446 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054717 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "568011de-56c0-55f4-95ec-0a426cbf6b67" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Central Angle Given the Coordinates of a Point on the Unit Circle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5b2fe475-6257-5ccc-8db9-274c6fa55fca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{45}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534447} {:answer/id #uuid "72d91a6d-fd5d-5373-9a45-1598ad4a0d34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{75}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534508} {:answer/id #uuid "7a9d26f0-861d-5a0a-95be-6706bd54fd00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{50}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534528} {:answer/id #uuid "95f4279b-1934-5ce0-b76b-a852503dc885" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{40}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534594} {:answer/id #uuid "f104ac01-a346-5997-bc8f-5574e571e0fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{30}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534802}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5b2fe475-6257-5ccc-8db9-274c6fa55fca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{45}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534447} :answer-field/id #uuid "ad7b8292-2cc9-59fa-8427-59bb85b1cab1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186053913}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "58adfdfb-8a6a-5b73-a9ed-33d397b103fb" :question/math-academy-id "q-64339" :question/problem "![](images/19/198f3a8a98e9650cf53c81a24a4fc96e08a743bdd6c48e47eb4b59f5bc8e4f2c.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $x$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},$ what is the measure of the angle $\\theta ?$" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "![](images/5b/5b8cfbcd58092a40f34ed969e9772981615db54eb5c20c5b7543e038a6e0fade.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $x=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nWe compute the angle $\\theta $ using the inverse cosine:\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})={45}^{∘}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534980 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054717 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "568011de-56c0-55f4-95ec-0a426cbf6b67" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Central Angle Given the Coordinates of a Point on the Unit Circle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7a65c10e-2ee7-5335-8ef2-65ee05b05946" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{81.46}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534527} {:answer/id #uuid "7b011649-9a23-53a3-90b7-1f25d2c60a71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{80.41}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534529} {:answer/id #uuid "83e6ddaf-77b4-5a0e-949f-91b022125de9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{78.45}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534551} {:answer/id #uuid "8f8402ff-e7d0-5cd5-a863-849e76e4cb95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{79.24}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534583} {:answer/id #uuid "b159daeb-e124-57f2-997e-d4b7b1075560" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{83.15}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534661}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7b011649-9a23-53a3-90b7-1f25d2c60a71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{80.41}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534529} :answer-field/id #uuid "8015e10c-af0f-5b56-a09c-f0c2ba72879d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534900}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "132171d1-1085-585d-b8f2-7700ff87a0d2" :question/math-academy-id "q-64521" :question/problem "![](images/2b/2b7a0fe3a80bfa6a0e0d82ab46a94740ce4b73007ae9d17ceb83170bce4559ff.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. The $x$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\frac{1}{6}.$ Rounded to two decimal places, what is the measure of the angle $\\theta ?$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/ea/eaf0e0a6434b454b58a8f53de0445bff8bae14d8bd1ec9e8e9598f0bd80323d9.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $x=\\frac{1}{6}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{6}.\n$$\n\nWe compute the angle $\\theta $ using the inverse cosine:\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{1}{6})=(80.405\\,93…)^{∘}≈{80.41}^{∘}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534990 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054719 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "83a4297b-016b-5182-bf4f-cd2b64a23c41" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Tangent or Cotangent of a Central Angle Given a Point on the Unit Circle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "50518fe9-0b2d-5dcc-89cd-eefab837bf7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534422} {:answer/id #uuid "5091e3af-a0e1-5296-ba59-d8a3f4c4675f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534424} {:answer/id #uuid "b9c28105-c9bd-57cf-88ff-3460736a6d61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534680} {:answer/id #uuid "f980b97a-e2c0-55f1-af77-7659b8749bf0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534817} {:answer/id #uuid "ff578584-d99e-5654-8c41-d355a3df44de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534825}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b9c28105-c9bd-57cf-88ff-3460736a6d61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534680} :answer-field/id #uuid "cd0b2e2a-2447-58b4-9753-a6af8b018534" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534944}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "288e585e-09b6-54a4-9e8e-a55aaefc8005" :question/math-academy-id "q-64981" :question/problem "![](images/cb/cbff0c974c790ec4287be1dcff8aa62d0da0aa5e77fa5ec577633f5f2cad6a5d.png)\n\nThe point $P(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\frac{1}{2})$ lies on the unit circle, as shown. What is $\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta ?$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/1f/1ffb0a83abf7e736c47035f4f337c36a6bb129fd0d64fe0ac3bfe8e46c194a11.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $x=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$ and $y=\\frac{1}{2}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nKnowing that $\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta },$ we can calculate $\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta =\\frac{(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})}{(\\frac{1}{2})}=\\sqrt{3}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534971 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054718 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8487f45b-a734-5375-abc2-5a347d49587c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Reciprocal Trigonometric Ratio Given the Coordinates of a Point on the Unit Circle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0dc2fd04-1bfc-5280-a69c-4ed2cd156d0a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534292} {:answer/id #uuid "68b38f75-3590-5f6c-b34f-83c73c9fd836" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4\\sqrt{7}}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534479} {:answer/id #uuid "6be28734-ccd4-54e1-836e-45a57ad99216" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4\\sqrt{3}}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534488} {:answer/id #uuid "6c50d7fa-7bb2-54b7-b910-ec04e9ed7eb5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4\\sqrt{7}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534491} {:answer/id #uuid "b5b1adc9-c4cd-505a-b89f-57bdded59b80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\sqrt{7}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534674}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "68b38f75-3590-5f6c-b34f-83c73c9fd836" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4\\sqrt{7}}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534479} :answer-field/id #uuid "1872fb4f-820b-5e4c-a4ea-11325b8f3aa5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534845}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "01f21d28-bd9a-5f49-bee9-224def342dbf" :question/math-academy-id "q-84641" :question/problem "![](images/11/11b00ddb6eb89e6acf79a8d5dc32e07a90850b97d8802df293e1240f6cfe444a.png)\n\nThe point $P(\\frac{3}{4},\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{4})$ lies on the unit circle, as shown. What is $\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta ?$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/cd/cd63145c6f0513fe78da148b07064eb725c2de88ded4993dabccc0da173d6583.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $y=\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{4}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{4}.\n$$\n\nKnowing that $\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta },$ we can calculate $\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{4})}=\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{7}}=\\frac{4\\sqrt{7}}{7}.\n$$"}]}
